Clean on the first attempt and re-confirmed after every subsequent edit.
`tsc -b` strict mode (`noUnusedLocals` / `noUnusedParameters` /
`noFallthroughCasesInSwitch`) passes. No new dependency added — `package.json`
unchanged.

## Files changed

- `src/lib/api.ts` — `RunEvent`'s `proposed` / `rejected` / `executed`
  variants gain `branch: string | null` (always present, never optional,
  per the contract's "not to be reinterpreted" instruction).
- `src/lib/events.ts` — `AxisStep` gains `branch: string | null` (set from
  the corresponding `RunEvent` field on every one of the three variants,
  not just `proposed`, since replay-order isn't guaranteed to start there)
  and `receivedAt: number` (client wall-clock captured once, at first
  observation of the step — powers a running lane's honest elapsed-time
  reading, since `remote_ms` only exists after a receipt lands). New pure
  helpers, all operating on the existing flat `axis.steps` map rather than
  a new stored structure (deliberately simpler than the design doc's
  speculative "new per-branch bucketing structure" — see "Judgment calls"):
  `axisBranches`, `hasFanout`, `isA2aAction`, `laneKind`,
  `countDelegationAttempts`.
- `src/features/runs/StepCard.tsx` — new optional `opaqueRemote` prop.
  When the step is still `proposed` (no receipt, no rejection) and
  `opaqueRemote` is set, renders the existing `Spinner` (imported from
  `components/ui/button.tsx`, already used for button loading states) +
  "waiting on remote agent…" instead of the plain "proposed…" text — a
  genuinely indeterminate treatment, never a percentage. Also: the executed
  state line now appends `· {n}s remote` when the result carries a numeric
  `remote_ms` (present on A2A results only; guarded with a narrow type
  check since `result` is `unknown`).
- `src/features/runs/FanoutLanes.tsx` (new) — the swimlane region. Renders
  nothing (`null`) when the axis has no branch-tagged steps — additive,
  zero visual change to an ordinary run. Each lane reuses `StepCard`
  unmodified; a lane's "kind" badge (`⇄ delegate` / `☰ review` / `⚙ tool`)
  is the only new markup per lane.
- `src/features/runs/Timeline.tsx` — flat step list now filters to
  `branch === null` (so a branch-tagged step never renders twice); renders
  `<FanoutLanes axis={axis} now={now} />` after the flat list. No change to
  the milestone-announcement effect — it already iterates every axis step
  regardless of `branch`, so lane rejections/executions are announced for
  free (see a11y below).
- `src/features/runs/MasterList.tsx` — new `FanoutBadge` component
  (subscribes to the run's axis via the existing `useAxis` hook; renders a
  small "⑂" glyph + `title` + `sr-only` text when `hasFanout` is true).
- `apps/web-ui/design/03-screens/R-3-live-timeline.md` — new "Parallel
  variant" section appended after the existing sequential spec (extends,
  does not replace).

## Rendered states (textual description — see "Honest limitations")

**Fan-out running, mixed lane states.** The detail pane shows the ordinary
run header (task, live badge, elapsed, Stop) unchanged. Below the (empty)
flat step area, a bordered "⑂ Fan-out — 3 branches" region appears with a
"2 delegations so far" chip (no denominator) at its top-right. Inside, a
left rail groups three lane rows in step-index order: an `⇄ delegate` lane
mid-flight shows a spinning indeterminate icon + "waiting on remote
agent…", no percentage anywhere on it; a second `⇄ delegate` lane already
has a receipt and shows "validated ✓ · executed · 1.8s remote" in success
green; a `⚙ tool` lane shows the same "validated ✓ · executed" line
`StepCard` already renders for any ordinary tool step, unchanged.

**One lane governance-refused.** That lane's `StepCard` shows the identical
danger-tinted rejection chip used for any other kernel rejection —
"Kernel rejected this step: delegation budget exhausted (10/10 this run) ·
retry 0" — same component, same copy shape, only the reason string differs.
Per ADR 0010's fail-fast stance (mirrored, not re-litigated here), the
whole plan run then fails; the run header transitions to the terminal
Result face like any other failed run, no special-cased "governance
failure" screen.

**One lane opaque-remote (isolated).** Same as the "running" lane above:
spinner, "waiting on remote agent…", no fabricated step count. If that
lane's delegation eventually errors instead of timing out silently, it
renders through `executed-failed` ("ran, tool error ✕") — the kernel has
no distinct "timed out" signal today (grepped, not found), so no fabricated
"timed-out" state was invented for the UI to display; this is a scope
simplification against the design doc's requirements line, documented
under "Judgment calls."

**All done.** All lanes show a settled state (`executed` success/failure or
`rejected`); the fan-out region stays visible as the historical record of
the plan run (mirrors how the flat timeline keeps completed step cards
visible after a run finishes) until the pane transitions to the terminal
Result face.

**Master-list fan-out indicator.** A run row that has (this session)
observed any branch-tagged event shows a small blue "⑂" glyph between the
status glyph and the task title, with a tooltip "This run forked into
parallel branches" and `sr-only` "forked" text for screen readers — same
visual weight and convention as the existing status glyphs.

I did **not** have a live daemon with the backend's ADR 0022 changes
available to screenshot against (the backend half was implemented
concurrently, in the same pass, and the two were not wired together for
this evidence pass) — the above is the same honest textual-description
fallback used for the ADR 0021 frontend round, not a fabricated screenshot
claim.

## Manual accessibility pass (no automated a11y tooling in this repo —
confirmed absent in the ADR-0021 round; not re-verified, cited)

- **Keyboard:** `FanoutLanes` and `MasterList`'s `FanoutBadge` introduce no
  new interactive controls — every clickable affordance in the fan-out view
  (a lane's own expand/collapse) is `StepCard`'s existing real `<button
  aria-expanded>`, already keyboard-operable and already carrying a visible
  focus ring (`focusRing` utility, mandatory offset per design/08 B-1a).
  The badges (kind, governance counter, fan-out indicator) are decorative
  `<span>`s, correctly non-focusable.
- **`aria-live` on lane status changes:** no new live region was added —
  the timeline's existing milestone-throttled `role="log"` announcer
  (`Timeline.tsx`'s announcement effect) iterates the FULL axis step list,
  which already includes branch-tagged steps; a lane's rejection or
  execution is announced through the same polite/assertive milestones as
  any ordinary step, verified by reading the effect's loop (it has no
  `branch` filter). This satisfies the hard constraint without inventing a
  parallel announcement path.
- **B-7 (glyph + text + color, never color alone):** kind badges (`⇄`/`☰`/
  `⚙` + word), the fan-out indicator (`⑂` + tooltip + `sr-only` text), and
  the governance-counter chip (plain text, no color-only signal) all follow
  this; the opaque-remote spinner is paired with literal "waiting on remote
  agent…" text, never motion-only.
- **Reduced motion:** the fan-out region's entrance animation
  (`animate-slide-in`) and the spinner's `animate-spin` are both plain CSS
  `animation` properties, already caught by design/05's global
  `@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)` wildcard override — no new
  motion-handling code needed, verified by reading that rule (`*,
  *::before, *::after`).

## Governance-counter design decision

Rendered as "**N delegations so far**" — a plain count, no denominator.
Grepped `crates/api/src/lib.rs`'s route table before deciding: no endpoint
exposes `DelegationLimits.max_delegations_per_run` (the design doc's own
Part 0/3 don't promise one either — only a `KernelConfig`/daemon-config
field, never an HTTP surface). The design doc's Part 6 explicitly gave two
options and told the implementer to default to NOT hardcoding a number the
backend hasn't promised to expose; that is exactly what happened here.
Depth was considered and dropped from the counter too: even if the backend
adds `a2a_depth` to the `Run` struct (Part 3), rendering "depth D" with no
`max_depth` counterpart is equally ungrounded, and the Part 6 Contract
section does not promise depth exposure at all (only `branch` on
`RunEvent`) — so it was left out rather than half-added.

## Honest limitations / gaps found during grounding

1. **No A2A trust concept exists anywhere in this codebase.** Grepped
   `crates/a2a/src/*.rs` and `crates/api/src/lib.rs` for `trust` — the only
   hit is `SkillView.trust` (skills, unrelated). A2A's security control is
   SSRF-pinned origin allowlisting (`A2aClient::allowed_origin`), not a
   trust label. The design brief's instruction to "mirror the skills
   trust-badge convention" for A2A lanes assumed data that does not exist.
   **No trust badge renders on A2A lanes** — fabricating one would violate
   the same honesty convention the badge is supposed to embody. Documented
   in the R-3 doc's a11y section as a disclosed gap, not silently absorbed.
2. **Master-list fan-out indicator is session-scoped, not authoritative.**
   It reflects only branch-tagged events the client has actually observed
   (live WS this session, or a prior in-session replay) — a completed
   fan-out run from before the current session, never opened, will not
   show the badge. No backend field answers "did this run fork" from the
   run-list endpoint alone. Stated plainly in both this log and the R-3 doc
   rather than worked around with a guess.
3. **No distinct "timed-out" lane state.** The requirements list asked for
   running/done/failed/timed-out per lane; grepping the kernel found no
   timeout concept distinct from an ordinary execution failure or a
   governance rejection, so no fourth fabricated state was added — a timed-
   out delegation renders as `executed-failed`, same as any other tool
   error, until/unless the kernel actually grows a distinct timeout signal.

## Judgment calls (for Eryk)

- `AxisState` did **not** get the "new per-branch bucketing structure" the
  design doc speculated it might need (Part 1). Each plan node maps 1:1 to
  exactly one axis step (the engine assigns one index per node — P1's
  concurrency changes *when* things run, not the step-keying model), so a
  swimlane is just the branch-tagged subset of the existing flat
  `axis.steps` map, filtered and sorted. Simpler than speculated, and kept
  that way deliberately rather than building unneeded structure.
  See `axisBranches` in `src/lib/events.ts`.
- No DAG/diagram renderer was built for "diverge/converge" — a bordered
  region + left rail + topological (step-index) ordering was judged
  proportionate to this app's dense, non-graphical visual language (nothing
  else in the app renders a graph). If a future round wants an actual
  branching diagram, this is a from-scratch addition, not an extension of
  what exists here.
- `StepCard`'s `opaqueRemote` prop is generic (not fan-out-specific) so a
  future single-delegation step-mode run could opt into the same honest
  indeterminate treatment without duplicating the spinner logic — not
  wired up for step-mode in this pass (out of the stated scope), but the
  seam is there.

