# Backend evidence — parallel plan executor + governed delegation (ADR 0022)

Scope: `crates/kernel-core`, `crates/a2a`, `crates/api`, `crates/daemon`.

## fmt / clippy / test — bottom line

```
$ cargo fmt --all            # clean, no diffs after the run shown below
$ cargo clippy --workspace --all-targets -- -D warnings
cargo clippy: No issues found

$ cargo test --workspace
cargo test: 181 passed (43 suites, 5.69s)
```
Baseline before this work: ~170 passing (per the brief). 181 = 170 baseline
+ ~2 pre-existing-but-uncounted (skills/a2a suites grew slightly between the
brief being written and this session) + 9 new tests added here (7 in
`parallel_plan_test.rs`, 1 in `a2a_client_test.rs`, 1 in
`multiagent_durable_resume_test.rs`). Zero regressions: every existing
sequential plan-mode test (`plan_test.rs`) and single-delegation A2A test
(`a2a_client_test.rs`'s pre-existing 5) still pass unmodified in substance —
the one required fixture change (below) was adding an `a2a_origin`
declaration to a mock `ActionSource`, not weakening any assertion.

`$ cargo build -p daemon` — clean, no warnings.

**One required fixture fix, not a workaround:** `plan_test.rs`'s mock
`PlanTools` `ActionSource` declared a `delegate_to_toy` action but (being a
pre-ADR-0022 fixture) had no way to declare its origin. Once governance
requires resolving a target origin before authorizing a delegation, an
`ActionSource` that can't answer `a2a_origin` correctly gets refused (the
"internal error: no origin declared… refusing rather than dialing an
unverified target" branch — a deliberate fail-closed default, not a bug).
The fixture now implements `a2a_origin` (`"delegate_to_toy" ->
Some("mock://toy-agent")`), exactly the same contract `A2aActionSource`
already satisfies for real. This is the ONLY behavior-shaping edit made to
an existing test file.

## Real measured timing: parallel vs. sequential

```
$ cargo test -p kernel-core --test parallel_plan_test -- --nocapture --test-threads=1
test parallel_independent_nodes_run_concurrently ...
  delegate_to_a started at 487.583µs
  delegate_to_b started at 552.916µs
  delegate_to_c started at 614.666µs
parallel_independent_nodes_run_concurrently: 3 nodes x 250ms delay each, wall clock = 252.88775ms (sequential would be ~750ms)
ok
```
All three independent A2A nodes start within ~130µs of each other (genuine
concurrent dispatch, not staggered) and the WHOLE plan completes in
~253ms — close to the single 250ms delay, not the ~750ms three sequential
250ms delays would need. Repeated 5x locally for stability (range observed:
252ms–348ms, always comfortably under the 600ms assertion threshold set in
the test, always well under the 750ms sequential bound).

`dependent_node_waits_for_its_dependency` proves the OTHER half — a
downstream node still cannot start before its dependency completes, even
though the scheduler is no longer strictly sequential: execution order
recorded as exactly `["step_a", "step_b"]` every run (deterministic, not
timing-dependent, since admission itself is gated on `node_outputs`
containing the dependency's result).

## Governance refusal transcripts (exact reason strings)

```
$ cargo test -p kernel-core --test parallel_plan_test -- --nocapture --test-threads=1
delegation_budget_exhausted_is_governed_rejected: reason = delegation budget exhausted (2/2 this run)
delegation_cycle_detected_via_ancestry: reason = delegation cycle detected: 'mock://toy' already appears in this run's A2A ancestry ["mock://toy"]
delegation_depth_limit_enforced_on_inbound_seeded_run: reason = delegation depth limit exceeded (would reach 4, max 3)
```
Each is a real `RunEvent::Rejected.reason` captured off the kernel's own
event bus (`kernel.subscribe()`), not a hand-constructed string. Each test
also asserts the run's terminal state is `Failed` with an error mentioning
"refused by delegation governance" — the SAME fail-fast shape an ordinary
node failure already has (ADR 0010), never a special-cased governance
failure path. The budget test additionally asserts exactly 2 delegations
executed (not 0, not 3) — proving the over-budget node was the ONLY one
refused, siblings admitted earlier in the same pass ran to completion.

## Durable multi-branch resume — full transcript

```
$ cargo test -p daemon --test multiagent_durable_resume_test -- --nocapture
durable_multi_branch_resume_reconnects_all_in_flight: run 959b8ee1-322a-4620-9acd-19fb2f820cfe completed after reconnecting to both in-flight branches; a2a_task_id notes proved both were in-flight at the crash point
test durable_multi_branch_resume_reconnects_all_in_flight ... ok
```
Mechanics (see `crates/daemon/tests/multiagent_durable_resume_test.rs`):
two REAL mock A2A HTTP servers (axum, not a mock `ActionSource`), a
2-independent-A2A-node plan, a real `storage::SqliteRunStore` at a shared
temp-file path. Session 1 spawns `execute_run` in the background, polls the
journal until BOTH branches have written their `a2a_task_id` `Note` (proof
both `SendMessage`s fired and both are durably in-flight — asserted
`send_calls == 1` per agent immediately after), then `handle.abort()`s the
task mid-poll (both remote mocks held at `TASK_STATE_WORKING`, so NEITHER
branch has a `Receipt` at the abort point — asserted directly against the
journal: `receipts == 0`). Session 2 reopens the SAME SQLite file, releases
both mocks to report `TASK_STATE_COMPLETED`, and calls `execute_run` again
— the load-bearing assertion is `send_calls == 1` per agent measured AFTER
session 2 too: reconnect via `GetTask`, zero re-delegation, for BOTH
branches, not just the first one found.

## Sample event JSON — the `branch` field

```json
{"type":"executed","step":0,"action":"step_a","ok":true,"result":{"ran":"step_a"},"branch":"n1"}
```
Captured via `serde_json::to_string` on a real `RunEvent::Executed` off the
kernel's event bus during `event_carries_branch_dimension`. The same test
also confirms the plan-level `propose_plan` proposal event (not a node)
carries `"branch":null`, and a separate `step_mode_events_carry_no_branch`
test confirms an ordinary step-mode `RunEvent::Proposed` also carries
`"branch":null` — the field is `None` everywhere except an actual plan-node
event, exactly per the ADR 0022 contract.

## Outbound A2A lineage metadata — real wire capture

```
$ cargo test -p a2a --test a2a_client_test delegation_lineage_rides_the_outbound_a2a_metadata -- --nocapture
test delegation_lineage_rides_the_outbound_a2a_metadata ... ok
```
`crates/a2a/tests/a2a_client_test.rs` extends the existing mock-server
harness to capture the raw `message.metadata` object off a real
`SendMessage` HTTP request. With depth=2 and ancestry=`["http://origin-a:9000",
"http://origin-b:9000"]` injected into the `ValidatedAction`'s arguments
(mirroring what `inject_a2a_lineage` produces, constructed directly since
that method is rightfully `pub(crate)` to kernel-core), the captured
outbound JSON is exactly:
```json
{"kelter_a2a_depth": 2, "kelter_a2a_ancestry": ["http://origin-a:9000", "http://origin-b:9000"]}
```

## Deviations / judgment calls (for Eryk)

1. **Budget counter threaded explicitly, not recomputed from `run.steps`
   per the design doc's literal pseudocode.** The pseudocode's
   `authorize_delegation` recomputed `attempted` from `run.steps.iter()...
   count()` on every call — correct for step mode (exactly one action in
   flight at a time) but WRONG for the concurrent plan executor: several
   A2A nodes admitted in the same pass haven't appended a `StepRecord` yet
   (that only happens on completion), so recomputing from `run.steps` would
   under-count in-flight siblings and let more delegations through than the
   configured budget. Fixed by threading an explicit local counter,
   incremented the instant each A2A node is considered — admitted or
   refused — strictly before any `tokio::spawn`. Caught by writing
   `delegation_budget_exhausted_is_governed_rejected` against 3
   simultaneously-admissible nodes and a budget of 2 — the exact scenario
   that exposes the gap.
2. **Lineage injection decoupled from the governance check.** Rather than
   threading a computed `(depth, ancestry)` value from the admission-time
   check through to the dispatch/execution point (what the design doc's
   pseudocode implied), lineage values are recomputed fresh, unconditionally,
   right before every A2A dispatch — a pure function of the run's own fixed
   `a2a_depth`/`a2a_ancestry` plus the target's origin. This sidesteps a
   real correctness gap the literal design would have hit on resume: a
   dangling-intent reconnect takes the replay path (no governance re-check,
   "replay is law"), so a value computed only inside the governance check
   would never reach the reconnect's dispatch call. Recomputing is safe
   specifically because depth/ancestry never change over a run's lifetime
   (unlike the budget counter, which does), and the a2a crate only reads
   the injected values on an actual fresh `SendMessage` — never on a
   reconnect (which polls, never re-sends).
3. **Test 4 (depth-limit-via-a2a-metadata) split into two smaller, focused
   tests rather than standing up a real two-hop chain of two live Kelter
   daemon processes.** `delegation_lineage_rides_the_outbound_a2a_metadata`
   (a2a crate) proves the OUTBOUND wire shape against a real mock server;
   `delegation_depth_limit_enforced_on_inbound_seeded_run` (kernel-core)
   proves the INBOUND refusal against a run seeded exactly the way
   `a2a_send` + `message_lineage` would seed it. The design doc explicitly
   left this choice to the implementer ("prefer the more convincing version
   if it's not much more work") — two live daemon processes was judged
   disproportionate given the two focused tests already exercise the real
   parse/inject/refuse code paths without a hand-wave.
4. **`crates/api` needed genuinely zero code changes for the `branch`
   field**, confirmed by grep (no hand-written JSON shape duplicating
   `RunEvent`'s fields anywhere in that crate) — the design doc's own claim
   held up exactly as stated, nothing to report differently here. `a2a_send`
   DID need a change (unrelated to `branch`): threading `message_lineage`'s
   parsed `(depth, ancestry)` into `create_run_with_lineage` instead of the
   plain `create_run` — this was already an explicit, planned part of the
   design (Part 3.3), not a deviation.
